{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a captivating film that delves into the origins of facebook and the complex journey of its founder mark zuckerberg. directed by david fincher, the movie offers a glimpse into the world of silicon valley startups and the intense, often cutthroat environment of technology entrepreneurship. the narrative is fast-paced and engaging, keeping the audience on the edge of their seats as it explores the legal battles, personal conflicts, and ethical dilemmas that surrounded the creation of facebook.\n\nthe performances in the film are outstanding, particularly jacob elordi as mark zuckerberg. elordi delivers a nuanced portrayal of a brilliant yet sometimes socially awkward individual, capturing the essence of his character's evolution from a college student to a tech mogul. other notable performances include andrew garfield as Eduardo saverin, mark's best friend and initial business partner, and jesse Eisenberg as himself, playing a pivotal role as one of the early investors in facebook.\n\nthe screenplay, penned by anthony mccarten, is sharp and witty, filled with dialogue that is both intellectually stimulating and emotionally resonant. it effectively balances the technical aspects of facebook's development with the human drama that unfolds behind the scenes. the film also does an excellent job of portraying the impact of facebook on society, raising important questions about privacy, ownership, and the power of social media.\n\nvisually, the social network is stunning. the cinematography by erik valente captures the essence of the tech world, from the vibrant energy of harvard to the sterile offices of silicon valley. the film's editing by kenneth l. bachor is seamless, enhancing the overall pacing and tension.\n\nwhile the movie takes some creative liberties with the true story, it remains a compelling and thought-provoking p